<p>Hemoglobin, when <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="d82fcb9d1f16927771836a9f6ccf14">bound</span> to <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="8d8151fa671692777188a59bbec61f">haptoglobin</span> or other proteins, becomes too large to pass through the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="83d76f27051692777202029aee0cd3">filtration</span> barrier. <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="ad2073fe6716927771885f3c1ec652">Hemoglobin</span> is a complex protein with a <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="b2df2436bf1692777193d90e9c0132">molecular weight</span> of about 64,500 Daltons. The <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="1ad1e71a4a1692777188853097f685">glomerular</span> <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="83d76f27051692777202029aee0cd3">filtration</span> barrier in the kidneys allows the passage of small molecules and ions but restricts the movement of large proteins and cells. <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="ad2073fe6716927771885f3c1ec652">Hemoglobin</span> is too large to pass through the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="83d76f27051692777202029aee0cd3">filtration</span> barrier, so it is retained within the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="a813884d6016927771838d2d1f9d1d">bloodstream</span> and does not get filtered into the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587">renal</span> tubules.</p>
<p><strong>It gets attracted to the negative charge of the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="1ad1e71a4a1692777188853097f685">glomerular</span> <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="83d76f27051692777202029aee0cd3">filtration</span> barrier (Option B):</strong> The <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="1ad1e71a4a1692777188853097f685">glomerular</span> <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="83d76f27051692777202029aee0cd3">filtration</span> barrier does have negative charges due to the presence of glycoproteins in the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="1ad1e71a4a1692777188853097f685">glomerular</span> basement membrane. These negative charges repel negatively charged molecules, preventing them from being filtered. However, <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="ad2073fe6716927771885f3c1ec652">hemoglobin</span> is not positively charged; its inability to pass through the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="76f619950f169277718830623b2798">glomerulus</span> is primarily due to its large size, not its charge.</p><br/><br/><div class="quiz-references">Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology, 14th edition, Pg 331</div>
